  "content": {
    "id": "p5-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "A three-digit number is reversed, and the original and reversed numbers are added. If the result is a perfect square, what could the original number be?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "For example, $110$.\n\nIts reverse is $011=11$, and $110+11=121=11^2$.",
      "worked": "Write the original number as $100a+10b+c$. Its reverse has value $100c+10b+a$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Sum}&=100a+10b+c+100c+10b+a\\\\\\text{Sum}&=101(a+c)+20b.\\end{align*}$$\nTaking $a=1$, $b=1$ and $c=0$ gives the original number $110$.\n$$\\begin{align*}110+11&=121\\\\110+11&=11^2.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $110$ is one possible original number.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
  },
